window.MARS_ACADEMY_CONTENT = {"version":"2.0.0","modules":[{"id":"godot","title":"Godot Orientation","subtitle":"Learn the editor as a system rather than a collection of mysterious panels.","lessons":[{"id":"godot-1","title":"Nodes, scenes, and the scene tree","summary":"The basic grammar of every Godot project.","concept":"A node is one object with one job. A scene is a saved tree of nodes that can be reused. The scene tree is the live hierarchy that determines ownership, transforms, lifecycle, and which objects can find one another. Think of a scene as a reusable machine: the root names the machine, child nodes provide capabilities, and scripts coordinate behavior.","keyPoints":["Prefer nodes with clear responsibilities.","Use scenes for reusable objects such as Player, Enemy, Weapon, or Arena.","Parent-child relationships affect transforms and lifecycle."],"example":"Player (CharacterBody3D)\n├── CollisionShape3D\n├── Visuals (Node3D)\n├── CameraRig (Node3D)\n└── CombatController (Node)","exercise":"Open Xogot or Godot and build the node tree above using placeholder nodes. Rename every node so its purpose is obvious.","check":{"q":"Which statement is most accurate?","options":["A scene is only a level","A node is one object; a scene is a reusable tree of nodes","Every node needs its own script","Children do not depend on parents"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Godot scenes are reusable node trees, not only levels."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"godot-2","title":"The editor panels","summary":"Know where to look before touching code.","concept":"The Scene dock shows the current node hierarchy. The Inspector exposes editable properties. The FileSystem dock shows imported project files. The 3D viewport edits spatial placement. Output and Debugger reveal runtime behavior. Most beginner confusion comes from using the correct concept in the wrong panel, so the first debugging question is often: am I editing the scene, the resource, the project setting, or the running instance?","keyPoints":["Scene dock: structure.","Inspector: data and node properties.","FileSystem: project files.","Debugger: runtime evidence."],"example":"Saved scene: what exists on disk\nRemote scene tree: what exists while the game is running","exercise":"Create a primitive MeshInstance3D. Change its transform in the Inspector, run the scene, then inspect it in the Remote scene tree.","check":{"q":"Where should you inspect a node that only exists while the game runs?","options":["Import dock","Remote scene tree","FileSystem dock","Shader editor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Remote scene tree shows runtime instances."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"godot-3","title":"Instancing and reusable scenes","summary":"Build once, reuse safely.","concept":"Instancing places one scene inside another. A Player scene can be instanced into an Arena scene without copying its internal nodes. Changes to the Player scene then propagate to its instances unless an instance property is intentionally overridden. This is the core of scalable Godot organization.","keyPoints":["Use an instance instead of duplicating a reusable object.","Keep reusable objects self-contained.","Avoid editing internal children from unrelated scenes when a public property or method would work."],"example":"Arena\n├── Player  ← instance of player.tscn\n├── Enemy   ← instance of enemy.tscn\n└── Environment","exercise":"Make a reusable TargetDummy scene, instance it three times, then change the source mesh and confirm all three update.","check":{"q":"Why is instancing better than copying a node tree repeatedly?","options":["It removes all dependencies","It keeps reusable objects linked to one source scene","It prevents properties from changing","It creates a separate project"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instances reuse one scene definition and reduce duplication."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"godot-4","title":"Project settings and input actions","summary":"Centralize rules that affect the whole game.","concept":"Project Settings stores engine-wide configuration such as physics tick rate, named input actions, display settings, and collision layer names. Gameplay code should read named actions such as attack_light or jump rather than physical buttons. This keeps controls remappable and prevents controller logic from being scattered through scripts.","keyPoints":["Use named actions, not hard-coded buttons.","Treat physics tick rate as a project rule.","Name collision layers before combat code depends on them."],"example":"if Input.is_action_just_pressed(\"jump\"):\n    try_jump()","exercise":"Create a test action named training_action and bind it to one controller button and one keyboard key. Print only when the action is first pressed.","check":{"q":"Why should gameplay scripts use named input actions?","options":["They allow remapping and centralize bindings","They make nodes invisible","They replace physics","They automatically animate characters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Named actions separate gameplay intent from physical device buttons."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"gdscript","title":"GDScript Foundations","subtitle":"Read, write, and reason about the language used to control the game.","lessons":[{"id":"gdscript-1","title":"Variables and types","summary":"Store state clearly and predictably.","concept":"Variables hold values that can change. Types describe what kind of value is allowed. Typed GDScript catches many mistakes before runtime and makes AI-generated code easier to inspect. Exported variables expose tuning values in the Inspector so game feel can be changed without editing logic.","keyPoints":["Use descriptive names.","Type important variables.","Export tuning values; keep internal state private by convention."],"example":"@export var move_speed: float = 6.0\nvar current_health: float = 100.0\nvar is_locked_on: bool = false","exercise":"Write five typed variables for a training fighter: health, speed, target, alive state, and attack name.","check":{"q":"What does @export primarily do?","options":["Runs a function every frame","Exposes a property in the Inspector","Creates a new scene","Connects a signal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"@export makes the property editable in the Inspector."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"gdscript-2","title":"Functions and guard clauses","summary":"Turn behavior into named operations.","concept":"A function groups a behavior behind a name. Parameters are inputs; return values are outputs. Guard clauses stop early when required conditions are not true. Short functions with explicit responsibilities are easier to debug than one function that handles input, movement, animation, damage, UI, and reset together.","keyPoints":["Name functions as actions or questions.","Use guard clauses to reject invalid actions early.","Avoid hidden side effects when a return value would be clearer."],"example":"func try_attack() -> bool:\n    if current_target == null:\n        return false\n    if is_staggered:\n        return false\n    begin_attack()\n    return true","exercise":"Write a function can_dodge() that returns false when dead, staggered, or already dodging.","check":{"q":"What is a guard clause?","options":["A function that draws a shield","An early return that rejects invalid conditions","A collision layer","A type of animation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guard clauses keep invalid paths from entering the rest of a function."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"gdscript-3","title":"Conditions, loops, arrays, and dictionaries","summary":"Control choices and collections.","concept":"Conditions choose which path runs. Loops repeat work. Arrays store ordered values; dictionaries map keys to values. In a combat game, an array might hold valid attacks or already-hit targets, while a dictionary might store body-region damage multipliers.","keyPoints":["Use arrays for ordered collections.","Use dictionaries for named lookup.","Do not loop over the entire scene tree when a group or direct reference will work."],"example":"var hit_targets: Array[Node] = []\nvar region_multiplier := {\n    \"head\": 1.35,\n    \"torso\": 1.0,\n    \"arm\": 0.75\n}","exercise":"Create a dictionary for three attack names and their damage, then loop through it and print each pair.","check":{"q":"Which structure is best for looking up damage by body-region name?","options":["Dictionary","Transform3D","Signal","SceneTreeTimer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A dictionary maps named keys to values."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"gdscript-4","title":"Lifecycle, delta, and physics","summary":"Know when code runs.","concept":"_ready() runs when a node enters the scene tree and is ready. _process(delta) runs every rendered frame. _physics_process(delta) runs at the fixed physics rate and should own CharacterBody3D movement and combat timing that must stay synchronized with physics. Delta is elapsed time in seconds, allowing values to change consistently across hardware.","keyPoints":["Initialize references in _ready().","Use _physics_process for movement and combat windows.","Multiply rates by delta; do not count rendered frames."],"example":"func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void:\n    attack_elapsed += delta\n    velocity.y -= gravity * delta\n    move_and_slide()","exercise":"Create a timer variable that reaches 1.0 second using delta, then resets and prints once.","check":{"q":"Why multiply a speed or rate by delta?","options":["To make behavior time-based instead of frame-count-based","To connect a signal","To create a resource","To rotate a UV map"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Delta converts a per-second rate into the amount for this update."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"architecture","title":"Architecture and Communication","subtitle":"Keep systems understandable enough to debug and extend.","lessons":[{"id":"arch-1","title":"Signals","summary":"Announce events without tightly coupling systems.","concept":"A signal says that something happened without requiring the sender to know every listener. A HealthComponent can emit died; a fight manager, UI, and animation controller may respond. Signals are strongest for events, not for continuously polling basic state.","keyPoints":["Sender emits; listeners connect.","Use signals for events such as damaged, died, equipped, or fight_started.","Disconnect or use lifecycle-safe connections when objects are replaced."],"example":"signal died(final_region: String)\n\nfunc take_damage(amount: float, region: String) -> void:\n    health -= amount\n    if health <= 0.0:\n        died.emit(region)","exercise":"Create a Button node that emits pressed and a separate Label that changes without the button script knowing the label exists.","check":{"q":"What problem do signals help solve?","options":["Event communication without direct dependency on every listener","UV unwrapping","Frame-rate limiting","Skeleton retargeting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Signals decouple event senders from receivers."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"arch-2","title":"Custom Resources","summary":"Put tunable data outside behavior scripts.","concept":"A Resource is a serializable data object. Custom Resources are ideal for attacks, weapons, movement tuning, AI tuning, and defense timing. One attack executor can run many attacks when each attack carries its own wind-up, active, recovery, damage, animation, and lunge values.","keyPoints":["Data describes what; controllers decide how.","Avoid scattering constants through scripts.","Resources can be shared, duplicated, and Inspector-edited."],"example":"class_name AttackData\nextends Resource\n\n@export var damage: float = 10.0\n@export var windup: float = 0.25\n@export var active: float = 0.12\n@export var recovery: float = 0.45","exercise":"Design a Resource on paper for a gladius light attack. Include at least six fields and explain why each belongs in data.","check":{"q":"What is the main benefit of attack Resources?","options":["New attacks can be configured without duplicating execution logic","They remove animations","They make physics optional","They replace collision masks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resources separate attack configuration from attack execution."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"arch-3","title":"Composition and responsibility","summary":"Build systems from focused parts.","concept":"Composition means an object gains capabilities from child nodes or helper objects rather than one enormous inheritance chain. A Combatant might contain health, movement, attack, defense, and animation components. Player input and enemy AI can then request actions through the same public combat interface.","keyPoints":["One component should have one dominant reason to change.","Player input and AI are separate brains.","Shared combat rules belong in reusable body components."],"example":"PlayerCombatant\n├── HealthComponent\n├── MovementController\n├── AttackController\n├── DefenseController\n└── AnimationDriver","exercise":"Take a hypothetical 1,000-line player script and list five components you would extract first.","check":{"q":"Which design best supports fair AI?","options":["AI directly edits enemy health and teleports","AI requests the same public actions available to player control","AI reads raw controller input","AI skips attack recovery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A shared action interface keeps both sides under the same rules."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"arch-4","title":"Explicit states","summary":"Make action exclusivity visible.","concept":"States represent mutually exclusive modes such as moving, attacking, defending, dodging, staggered, or dead. A state machine is useful when transitions matter and incompatible actions must not overlap. It should clarify behavior, not create a huge framework for simple cases.","keyPoints":["State answers: what can happen now?","Transitions need explicit conditions.","Keep movement intention separate from combat action state when useful."],"example":"enum ActionState { FREE, ATTACKING, DEFENDING, DODGING, STAGGERED, DEAD }\nvar action_state := ActionState.FREE","exercise":"Draw a transition map showing how FREE can enter ATTACKING, DODGING, or DEFENDING and how each returns.","check":{"q":"Why is an explicit DEAD state useful?","options":["It prevents ordinary combat actions after death","It creates textures","It improves Wi-Fi","It automatically exports Windows builds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A death state clearly shuts down incompatible behavior."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"math","title":"3D Math for Action Games","subtitle":"Learn only the math that directly controls movement, facing, targeting, and camera behavior.","lessons":[{"id":"math-1","title":"Vectors","summary":"Represent direction and magnitude.","concept":"A Vector3 contains x, y, and z components. It can represent position, direction, or velocity. Vector length is magnitude. Normalization keeps direction but changes length to 1, which prevents diagonal input from moving faster than straight input when full speed is intended.","keyPoints":["Subtract positions to get a direction.","Normalize when only direction should remain.","Preserve analog magnitude when stick pressure should affect speed."],"example":"var to_target: Vector3 = target.global_position - global_position\nvar distance: float = to_target.length()\nvar direction: Vector3 = to_target.normalized()","exercise":"Given player (2,0,3) and target (5,0,7), calculate the unnormalized direction vector.","check":{"q":"What does normalized() preserve?","options":["Direction while setting length to 1","Position while deleting rotation","Only the Y axis","Animation duration"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Normalization creates a unit direction vector."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"math-2","title":"Local and global space","summary":"Understand whose axes you are using.","concept":"Global coordinates describe the world. Local coordinates describe a value relative to a node’s parent or orientation. Camera-relative movement converts stick input into the camera’s flattened forward and right directions. Locked-on animation often converts world velocity back into the character’s local forward and right values.","keyPoints":["Global direction answers where in the world.","Local direction answers relative to this object.","Basis vectors expose an object’s oriented axes."],"example":"var forward := -camera.global_transform.basis.z\nforward.y = 0.0\nforward = forward.normalized()","exercise":"Rotate a Node3D 90 degrees and predict how its local forward differs from world forward.","check":{"q":"Why flatten camera forward for ground movement?","options":["Camera pitch should not reduce or add vertical ground movement","To disable controller input","To change mesh scale","To create a signal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ground input should use the horizontal projection of camera direction."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"math-3","title":"Dot product and angles","summary":"Measure alignment without guessing.","concept":"The dot product compares two normalized directions. A result near 1 means they point the same way, 0 means perpendicular, and -1 means opposite. It is useful for back attacks, target visibility, directional reactions, and deciding whether something is in front of a fighter.","keyPoints":["Normalize before interpreting dot as alignment.","Use thresholds rather than exact equality.","Signed angles preserve left-versus-right information."],"example":"var facing_dot := forward.normalized().dot(to_target.normalized())\nif facing_dot < -0.7:\n    apply_back_attack_bonus()","exercise":"Write three plain-language rules for dot values above 0.7, between -0.3 and 0.3, and below -0.7.","check":{"q":"A dot product near -1 means the directions are:","options":["Nearly opposite","Nearly identical","Both zero","Different lengths only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Normalized directions pointing opposite produce -1."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"math-4","title":"Interpolation and smoothing","summary":"Move toward targets without snapping.","concept":"Interpolation gradually changes a value. move_toward changes by a fixed maximum amount; lerp moves a percentage of the remaining distance. Smoothing is used for speed, turning, camera orbit, and target bias, but it must not delay critical gameplay timing or make controls feel disconnected.","keyPoints":["Use time-scaled rates.","Different systems need different smoothing.","Never hide bad state transitions behind excessive smoothing."],"example":"velocity.x = move_toward(velocity.x, desired.x, acceleration * delta)\nrotation.y = lerp_angle(rotation.y, desired_yaw, turn_speed * delta)","exercise":"Compare a fast acceleration and slow deceleration setup against the reverse. Describe the feel difference.","check":{"q":"Which function is designed for angles that wrap around?","options":["lerp_angle","print","emit_signal","queue_free"],"answer":0,"explanation":"lerp_angle handles angle wraparound correctly."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"movement","title":"Movement, Camera, and Lock-On","subtitle":"Build the physical foundation before combat.","lessons":[{"id":"move-1","title":"CharacterBody3D movement","summary":"Let the engine solve controlled character collision.","concept":"CharacterBody3D is intended for characters moved by code. You assign velocity and call move_and_slide in _physics_process. Ground detection, gravity, slopes, and collision behavior depend on the body’s collision shape and motion settings. Visual meshes should be children and should not own core movement logic.","keyPoints":["Move in _physics_process.","Use one authoritative velocity.","Keep visual animation separate from physical movement."],"example":"func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void:\n    if not is_on_floor():\n        velocity.y -= gravity * delta\n    velocity.x = desired.x\n    velocity.z = desired.z\n    move_and_slide()","exercise":"In Xogot, make a capsule body fall onto a floor and move using the left stick.","check":{"q":"Which node should own code-driven third-person collision movement?","options":["CharacterBody3D","Label","AudioStreamPlayer","WorldEnvironment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CharacterBody3D is designed for scripted character motion."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"move-2","title":"Acceleration, braking, and turning","summary":"Create weight without losing responsiveness.","concept":"Instant speed changes can feel synthetic, but too much acceleration makes controls sluggish. Separate acceleration, deceleration, and turning values allow different qualities. Distance traveled should visually agree with foot cadence, especially for in-place animation.","keyPoints":["Tune one value group at a time.","Use analog stick magnitude.","Judge movement using repeated starts, stops, reversals, and circles."],"example":"var target_velocity := move_direction * move_speed * stick_strength\nvelocity.x = move_toward(velocity.x, target_velocity.x, acceleration * delta)","exercise":"Create a test protocol: full-speed start, sudden release, 180-degree reversal, small-stick walk, and continuous circle.","check":{"q":"Why separate acceleration and deceleration?","options":["Starting and stopping may need different feel","To create UVs","To enable signals","To change physics tick rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Separate rates let starts and stops have different responsiveness and weight."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"move-3","title":"Third-person camera","summary":"Separate player orientation from view control.","concept":"A camera rig commonly uses a yaw pivot, pitch pivot, and camera boom or collision mechanism. Right-stick input changes yaw and pitch. Pitch is clamped. Camera collision shortens the camera distance when geometry obstructs the view and restores it smoothly when clear.","keyPoints":["Yaw is horizontal orbit.","Pitch is vertical orbit.","Camera collision should not move the player."],"example":"CameraRig\n└── YawPivot\n    └── PitchPivot\n        └── SpringArm3D\n            └── Camera3D","exercise":"Build the hierarchy above and test orbit before attaching it to a character.","check":{"q":"What is the job of a SpringArm3D in this setup?","options":["Help keep the camera out of geometry","Store attack damage","Retarget bones","Control enemy health"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A spring arm can shorten around obstructions."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"move-4","title":"Lock-on and combat positioning","summary":"Constrain the fighter without confiscating the camera.","concept":"Lock-on selects a valid target, makes the character face it, converts movement into strafing, and may apply soft framing assistance. The camera should retain player orbit control. Acquisition distance is normally smaller than break distance to avoid flickering at the boundary.","keyPoints":["Validate target distance and life state.","Use hysteresis: acquire tighter, break farther.","Character facing and camera orbit are separate systems."],"example":"if distance_to_target > lock_break_distance:\n    clear_lock_on()\nelse:\n    face_target(delta)","exercise":"Create a paper acceptance test covering acquire, orbit, strafe, target death, and distance break.","check":{"q":"Why make break distance larger than acquisition distance?","options":["To create stable hysteresis","To increase attack damage","To stop all camera input","To reduce texture size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The gap prevents rapid lock/unlock near one threshold."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"animation","title":"Animation Implementation","subtitle":"Make visuals obey gameplay without letting clips control the rules.","lessons":[{"id":"anim-1","title":"AnimationPlayer and libraries","summary":"Understand what is actually being played.","concept":"AnimationPlayer stores and plays clips from AnimationLibraries. Imported character files may contain multiple clips or one clip each. You need to know clip names, lengths, loop settings, track contents, and whether the root moves. Playback speed can retime a visual without changing gameplay duration.","keyPoints":["Inspect clip length and tracks.","Name clips consistently.","Do not assume imported defaults are correct."],"example":"animation_player.play(\"attack_light\")\nanimation_player.speed_scale = source_length / desired_duration","exercise":"Create an animation catalogue table with clip name, source, length, loop, root motion, and intended use.","check":{"q":"What should control combat active timing?","options":["Authored combat data","The visual clip length alone","The monitor refresh rate","The mesh polygon count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gameplay timing must remain authoritative."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"anim-2","title":"AnimationTree and blend spaces","summary":"Blend continuous locomotion cleanly.","concept":"AnimationTree coordinates states and blend values. A BlendSpace can combine forward, backward, and lateral locomotion according to local velocity. A state machine can switch between locomotion, airborne, attack, reaction, and death branches. Scripts should set meaningful parameters rather than restart clips every frame.","keyPoints":["Drive blends from local movement values.","Use damping where visual transitions need it.","Keep state transitions inspectable."],"example":"animation_tree.set(\"parameters/Locomotion/blend_position\", Vector2(local_x, local_z))","exercise":"On paper, place idle at the center of a 2D blend space and map forward, back, left, and right clips.","check":{"q":"What input is most useful for a locked-on locomotion blend space?","options":["Character-local lateral and forward velocity","Raw screen pixels","Enemy health only","File paths"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Local velocity describes movement relative to the character’s facing."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"anim-3","title":"Jump, one-shots, and interruptions","summary":"Handle animations that should play once.","concept":"Jump usually needs a takeoff, airborne loop, and landing response. Attacks and reactions are one-shot actions that temporarily override locomotion. Gameplay should not become locked for a two-second landing clip when the physical jump lasts less than a second; retime or select a better clip.","keyPoints":["Separate takeoff, airborne, and landing phases.","Allow gameplay-critical interruption rules to be explicit.","Measure physical duration before choosing visual timing."],"example":"takeoff_duration = 0.20\nairborne loops until grounded\nlanding_duration = 0.25","exercise":"Record the physical air time of a jump and propose visual durations that fit inside it.","check":{"q":"What is wrong with waiting for an overly long landing animation to finish?","options":["It can block control long after the physical action is over","It improves responsiveness","It fixes collision automatically","It reduces memory use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Visuals should fit the gameplay action, not extend it arbitrarily."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"anim-4","title":"Retargeting and import discipline","summary":"Prevent asset-pipeline problems from reaching combat code.","concept":"Retargeting maps animation bones from one humanoid skeleton to another. Rest pose, bone mapping, scale, orientation, and root-motion handling must be verified early. The simplest pipeline uses one consistent character rig and animations authored for it, reducing conversion and retargeting work.","keyPoints":["Verify one clip before bulk importing.","Establish scale once.","Do not patch bone or scale errors in gameplay scripts."],"example":"Import checklist:\n1. Correct scale\n2. Correct forward axis\n3. Skeleton maps\n4. Rest pose matches\n5. Clip loops correctly\n6. Root motion policy is known","exercise":"Create a six-item inspection form for every newly imported humanoid animation.","check":{"q":"When should a skeleton mismatch be discovered?","options":["As early as possible, before systems depend on it","After final combat polish","Only during export","It never matters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Early verification prevents expensive downstream rework."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"combat","title":"Combat Engineering","subtitle":"Turn deliberate timing and collision into a readable duel.","lessons":[{"id":"combat-1","title":"Attack phases","summary":"Represent commitment as data.","concept":"Every attack progresses through wind-up, active, and recovery. Optional chain and cancel windows sit inside that timeline. Wind-up telegraphs; active frames can hit; recovery creates vulnerability. A timer in the fixed physics update advances the action independently of render frame rate.","keyPoints":["Only active frames can deal damage.","Misses retain recovery.","Chain and cancel windows are explicit, not vague animation moments."],"example":"0.00 ─ wind-up ─ 0.24 ─ active ─ 0.36 ─ recovery ─ 0.82","exercise":"Use the Attack Timeline Lab to design a light and heavy attack with visibly different commitment.","check":{"q":"Which phase makes a missed attack punishable?","options":["Recovery","Active only","Idle","Import"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recovery preserves commitment after a miss."},"source":"Project combat specification"},{"id":"combat-2","title":"Hitboxes, hurtboxes, and one hit per swing","summary":"Make damage deterministic.","concept":"A weapon hitbox is enabled only during the attack’s active window. Hurtboxes mark valid target regions. Collision layers and masks prevent self-hits and unrelated contacts. Each swing tracks targets already hit so overlapping physics frames do not apply repeated damage.","keyPoints":["Clear hit history at attack start.","Disable hitbox outside active time.","Use collision contact to trigger authored reactions, not uncontrolled weapon physics."],"example":"func begin_attack() -> void:\n    hit_targets.clear()\n\nfunc _on_hitbox_area_entered(area: Area3D) -> void:\n    if area in hit_targets: return\n    hit_targets.append(area)\n    apply_hit(area)","exercise":"Use the Collision Lab to configure player weapon, enemy hurtbox, enemy weapon, player hurtbox, and guard volume.","check":{"q":"When should hit_targets be cleared?","options":["At the start of each new swing","Every frame","Only on death","Never"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each swing needs a fresh record while still preventing duplicate hits within that swing."},"source":"Project combat specification"},{"id":"combat-3","title":"Combat state, chains, and cancels","summary":"Control when one action may become another.","concept":"Input during a chain window may request the next attack. Input outside the window should not queue an entire sequence. Cancels into block or dodge may be allowed during selected wind-up or late-recovery periods but normally not during active hit frames. These rules create skill and prevent animation chaos.","keyPoints":["Queue at most the intended next action.","Make windows per attack.","Reject incompatible transitions before animation code runs."],"example":"if chain_window.contains(action_elapsed) and attack_pressed:\n    queued_attack = choose_contextual_attack()","exercise":"Design a transition table for attack → attack, attack → dodge, attack → block, and attack → stagger.","check":{"q":"Why forbid most cancels during active hit frames?","options":["To preserve attack commitment and readable rules","To change texture filtering","To improve Wi-Fi","To create collision shapes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Active frames are the committed striking portion."},"source":"Project combat specification"},{"id":"combat-4","title":"Timed defense and physical dodges","summary":"Create defense through timing and position.","concept":"Timed defense opens a short window. The earliest portion can produce a perfect result and the remaining portion a standard block. A dodge without invulnerability succeeds only if movement actually clears the attack. This makes collision, timing, and spacing honest and visible.","keyPoints":["Perfect and standard windows are separate.","Defense requires valid equipment under the project rules.","Dodge duration and distance must match animation and collision."],"example":"defense elapsed: 0.00 ─ perfect ─ 0.12 ─ standard ─ 0.30 ─ fail","exercise":"Use the Defense Reflex Lab for ten attempts, then change the window and explain how difficulty changes.","check":{"q":"Without invulnerability frames, what makes a dodge successful?","options":["Actual displacement outside the attack","A hidden immunity flag","The dodge animation name","Enemy input reading"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The character must physically avoid the hit."},"source":"Project combat specification"}]},{"id":"ai","title":"Enemy AI","subtitle":"Build an opponent that obeys the same world as the player.","lessons":[{"id":"ai-1","title":"Intention states","summary":"Separate deciding from performing.","concept":"AI intention states such as approach, circle, attack, defend, retreat, recover, staggered, and dead decide what the enemy wants. The shared combat system decides whether the requested action is valid and executes normal wind-up, hit, recovery, and damage rules.","keyPoints":["AI chooses intentions.","Combat controller validates actions.","Never let decision code bypass shared rules."],"example":"match ai_state:\n    APPROACH: request_move_toward_target()\n    ATTACK: combatant.try_attack(chosen_attack)\n    DEFEND: combatant.try_defend()","exercise":"Draw an AI state map that includes at least one exit from every state.","check":{"q":"What is the benefit of separating intention from execution?","options":["AI can use shared legal combat actions instead of cheating","It removes all states","It disables recovery","It makes attacks instantaneous"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Decision code chooses; shared combat code enforces rules."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"ai-2","title":"Distance bands and visible information","summary":"Make decisions from readable game state.","concept":"Distance bands provide simple context: too far means approach, preferred range may mean circle, attack range permits attacks, and danger states may trigger retreat. Fair AI observes positions, distances, current visible actions, recent outcomes, and health—not raw controller input or future button presses.","keyPoints":["Use editable thresholds.","Add hysteresis to avoid rapid state switching.","Log why a decision was chosen."],"example":"if distance > approach_distance:\n    set_state(APPROACH)\nelif player_is_attacking and can_block:\n    set_state(DEFEND)\nelse:\n    set_state(CIRCLE)","exercise":"Write five pieces of visible information the AI may use and three pieces it must not use.","check":{"q":"Which input is prohibited for fair AI?","options":["Player raw button input","Distance to player","Player visible attack state","Recent repeated action"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The AI should react to game state, not private raw input."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"ai-3","title":"Weighted action selection","summary":"Create variation without randomness replacing design.","concept":"Weights influence choices among currently valid actions. Distance, health, recent actions, and tactical role can modify weights. Invalid actions should be removed before random selection. Seeded randomness or logged rolls make debugging reproducible.","keyPoints":["Filter invalid actions first.","Weights express preference, not permission.","Avoid choosing every frame; commit to decisions."],"example":"valid = [LIGHT_ATTACK, CIRCLE, DODGE]\nweights = [0.55, 0.35, 0.10]","exercise":"Use the AI Decision Lab to create weights for normal, low-health, and player-airborne situations.","check":{"q":"What should happen before weighted selection?","options":["Remove actions that are not currently valid","Read future player inputs","Disable recovery","Set all weights equal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weighting should only choose among legal actions."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"ai-4","title":"Debugging combat AI","summary":"Make invisible decisions visible.","concept":"AI often appears “stupid” when it is actually blocked by a condition, stuck in a state, missing a target, or repeatedly reconsidering. A debug panel should display current intention, distance, chosen action, relevant timers, failed requests, and transition reasons.","keyPoints":["Show current state and previous state.","Log transition reason, not just state name.","Use a minimal arena to isolate AI problems."],"example":"AI: CIRCLE → ATTACK\nReason: distance 2.1m, attack_ready=true, roll=0.42\nRequest result: accepted","exercise":"Design a one-screen AI debug overlay with no more than eight lines.","check":{"q":"Which debug information is most useful?","options":["State transition reason","Only frame rate","Texture file size","Monitor brightness"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The reason reveals why the AI chose or failed to choose an action."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"blender","title":"Blender Pipeline","subtitle":"Create and repair game-ready assets without turning the year into an art degree.","lessons":[{"id":"blend-1","title":"Navigation and mesh modeling","summary":"Learn the minimum vocabulary of 3D creation.","concept":"Object Mode manipulates whole objects; Edit Mode changes mesh vertices, edges, and faces. Transforms, origins, normals, snapping, and modifiers determine whether an asset exports cleanly. Start with weapons, shields, rocks, and architectural props before humanoid characters.","keyPoints":["Apply or intentionally preserve transforms.","Check normals.","Model at real-world scale."],"example":"Practice asset order:\n1. Rock\n2. Pillar\n3. Shield\n4. Gladius\n5. Armor piece","exercise":"Model a low-detail parma shield from a cylinder, then add thickness and a central boss.","check":{"q":"Which mode edits vertices and faces?","options":["Edit Mode","Object Mode only","Pose Mode only","Render Mode"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Edit Mode changes mesh geometry."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"blend-2","title":"UVs and PBR materials","summary":"Tell textures where and how to appear.","concept":"UV coordinates flatten a 3D surface into 2D texture space. PBR materials commonly use base color, roughness, metallic, and normal information. Clean UV islands, sensible texel density, and correct roughness usually matter more than extreme texture resolution.","keyPoints":["Avoid overlapping UVs unless intentional.","Metallic is usually material identity, not a generic shine slider.","Roughness strongly controls visual readability."],"example":"Metal shield: metallic high, roughness varied\nCloth tunic: metallic zero, roughness high\nMars rock: metallic zero, roughness high","exercise":"Create three material recipes for gladius steel, leather grip, and dusty stone.","check":{"q":"Which property mainly controls how sharp or broad reflections appear?","options":["Roughness","Node name","Physics tick rate","Signal count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Roughness controls microsurface reflection spread."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"blend-3","title":"Armatures and weight painting","summary":"Understand why characters deform.","concept":"An armature is a bone hierarchy. Skinning binds mesh vertices to bones; weights control how much each bone influences each vertex. Poor weights cause collapsing elbows, stretched shoulders, or armor bending incorrectly. Learn the process on a simple rig before attempting a full original human.","keyPoints":["Rest pose matters.","Weights around joints must transition smoothly.","Rigid armor may need separate weighting or attachment."],"example":"Mesh vertex weight example:\nUpperArm bone: 0.70\nForearm bone: 0.30","exercise":"Rig a simple two-segment arm and test bending at several angles.","check":{"q":"What does weight painting define?","options":["Bone influence on vertices","Camera collision range","Attack recovery","Controller deadzone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weights determine mesh deformation under bones."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"blend-4","title":"Animation and export","summary":"Move assets through the pipeline deliberately.","concept":"Keyframes store values at points in time. The Dope Sheet organizes timing; the Graph Editor controls interpolation. Game export requires correct scale, axes, skeleton, clip ranges, and material references. Test one animation in Godot before creating many.","keyPoints":["Anticipation helps motion read.","Follow-through prevents robotic stops.","Export settings are part of the asset, not an afterthought."],"example":"Export check:\nScale 1.0\nCorrect forward direction\nOnly required objects\nAnimation ranges named\nMaterials linked","exercise":"Create a 20-frame shield-ready animation with anticipation and a settled final pose.","check":{"q":"Which editor is best for adjusting animation curves and easing?","options":["Graph Editor","File browser","UV editor only","Godot Output panel"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Graph Editor exposes interpolation curves."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]},{"id":"debug","title":"Debugging and Production Discipline","subtitle":"Turn confusion into a repeatable investigation.","lessons":[{"id":"debug-1","title":"Read errors as evidence","summary":"Start with what the engine actually reports.","concept":"Parser errors mean the script could not be understood. Runtime errors occur while the game runs. Stack traces show the call path. Read the first relevant error, open the exact file and line, inspect the value involved, and avoid changing unrelated systems until the fault is reproduced.","keyPoints":["Fix the earliest causal error first.","Differentiate parser, runtime, and logic errors.","Record exact reproduction steps."],"example":"ERROR: Invalid access to property \"global_position\" on a null instance.\nQuestion: Which reference is null, and when should it have been assigned?","exercise":"Take one old error message from your project and rewrite it as: evidence, hypothesis, test, result.","check":{"q":"What should you usually fix first when many errors appear?","options":["The earliest error likely causing later failures","The last cosmetic warning","All files at once","The texture with the largest size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Later errors may be consequences of the first failure."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"debug-2","title":"Minimal reproductions","summary":"Remove noise until the cause is visible.","concept":"A minimal reproduction is the smallest project or scene that still demonstrates the problem. Copy only the necessary nodes, script, input, and asset. If the bug disappears, one of the removed dependencies matters. This method is especially useful for animation import, collision, camera, and state-transition faults.","keyPoints":["One scene, one behavior, one visible failure.","Preserve the exact failing condition.","Do not “simplify” away the bug itself."],"example":"Full duel bug → test scene with one CharacterBody3D, one Area3D, one target, one attack","exercise":"Choose one known project issue and list what the minimal test scene must contain and what can be removed.","check":{"q":"What is the goal of a minimal reproduction?","options":["Isolate the smallest setup that still fails","Add every production system","Improve art quality","Avoid all testing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Isolation reduces the number of possible causes."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"debug-3","title":"Git and safe experiments","summary":"Make mistakes reversible.","concept":"Git records snapshots of project files. Commit a stable state before risky changes, use descriptive messages, and inspect diffs before accepting large AI edits. Generated imports and large licensed source files require appropriate ignore rules. Version control is not only backup—it is a reasoning tool.","keyPoints":["Commit coherent changes.","Review diffs.","Do not mix unrelated fixes into one commit."],"example":"git status\ngit diff\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"feat: add camera-relative movement\"","exercise":"Write five commit messages for foundation, movement, lock-on, attack timing, and AI debugging work.","check":{"q":"Why inspect a diff before committing AI-generated changes?","options":["To understand exactly what changed and catch unrelated edits","To retarget animations","To increase frame rate","To create a mesh"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Diff review keeps generated changes controlled and understandable."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"},{"id":"debug-4","title":"Acceptance tests and phase gates","summary":"Define done before polishing.","concept":"An acceptance test describes observable behavior. “Camera feels good” is too vague by itself; “camera never passes through the arena wall during ten full orbits” is testable. Strict phase gates prevent combat from being built on movement or lock-on that has not been manually verified.","keyPoints":["Tests describe actions and expected outcomes.","Separate automatic from manual tests.","Do not begin dependent systems before the gate passes."],"example":"Action: orbit camera against wall for 30 seconds\nExpected: no geometry clipping, no snapping, smooth recovery","exercise":"Write five acceptance tests for a single light attack.","check":{"q":"What makes an acceptance test useful?","options":["It describes observable action and expected result","It uses vague adjectives only","It avoids playtesting","It replaces version control"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Observable criteria make completion verifiable."},"source":"Core game-development knowledge"}]}],"practice":[{"id":"check-godot-1","category":"Godot Orientation","type":"choice","prompt":"Which statement is most accurate?","options":["A scene is only a level","A node is one object; a scene is a reusable tree of nodes","Every node needs its own script","Children do not depend on parents"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Godot scenes are reusable node trees, not only levels.","lessonId":"godot-1"},{"id":"check-godot-2","category":"Godot Orientation","type":"choice","prompt":"Where should you inspect a node that only exists while the game runs?","options":["Import dock","Remote scene tree","FileSystem dock","Shader editor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Remote scene tree shows runtime instances.","lessonId":"godot-2"},{"id":"check-godot-3","category":"Godot Orientation","type":"choice","prompt":"Why is instancing better than copying a node tree repeatedly?","options":["It removes all dependencies","It keeps reusable objects linked to one source scene","It prevents properties from changing","It creates a separate project"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instances reuse one scene definition and reduce duplication.","lessonId":"godot-3"},{"id":"check-godot-4","category":"Godot Orientation","type":"choice","prompt":"Why should gameplay scripts use named input actions?","options":["They allow remapping and centralize bindings","They make nodes invisible","They replace physics","They automatically animate characters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Named actions separate gameplay intent from physical device buttons.","lessonId":"godot-4"},{"id":"check-gdscript-1","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"choice","prompt":"What does @export primarily do?","options":["Runs a function every frame","Exposes a property in the Inspector","Creates a new scene","Connects a signal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"@export makes the property editable in the Inspector.","lessonId":"gdscript-1"},{"id":"check-gdscript-2","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"choice","prompt":"What is a guard clause?","options":["A function that draws a shield","An early return that rejects invalid conditions","A collision layer","A type of animation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guard clauses keep invalid paths from entering the rest of a function.","lessonId":"gdscript-2"},{"id":"check-gdscript-3","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"choice","prompt":"Which structure is best for looking up damage by body-region name?","options":["Dictionary","Transform3D","Signal","SceneTreeTimer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A dictionary maps named keys to values.","lessonId":"gdscript-3"},{"id":"check-gdscript-4","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"choice","prompt":"Why multiply a speed or rate by delta?","options":["To make behavior time-based instead of frame-count-based","To connect a signal","To create a resource","To rotate a UV map"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Delta converts a per-second rate into the amount for this update.","lessonId":"gdscript-4"},{"id":"check-arch-1","category":"Architecture and Communication","type":"choice","prompt":"What problem do signals help solve?","options":["Event communication without direct dependency on every listener","UV unwrapping","Frame-rate limiting","Skeleton retargeting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Signals decouple event senders from receivers.","lessonId":"arch-1"},{"id":"check-arch-2","category":"Architecture and Communication","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the main benefit of attack Resources?","options":["New attacks can be configured without duplicating execution logic","They remove animations","They make physics optional","They replace collision masks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resources separate attack configuration from attack execution.","lessonId":"arch-2"},{"id":"check-arch-3","category":"Architecture and Communication","type":"choice","prompt":"Which design best supports fair AI?","options":["AI directly edits enemy health and teleports","AI requests the same public actions available to player control","AI reads raw controller input","AI skips attack recovery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A shared action interface keeps both sides under the same rules.","lessonId":"arch-3"},{"id":"check-arch-4","category":"Architecture and Communication","type":"choice","prompt":"Why is an explicit DEAD state useful?","options":["It prevents ordinary combat actions after death","It creates textures","It improves Wi-Fi","It automatically exports Windows builds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A death state clearly shuts down incompatible behavior.","lessonId":"arch-4"},{"id":"check-math-1","category":"3D Math for Action Games","type":"choice","prompt":"What does normalized() preserve?","options":["Direction while setting length to 1","Position while deleting rotation","Only the Y axis","Animation duration"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Normalization creates a unit direction vector.","lessonId":"math-1"},{"id":"check-math-2","category":"3D Math for Action Games","type":"choice","prompt":"Why flatten camera forward for ground movement?","options":["Camera pitch should not reduce or add vertical ground movement","To disable controller input","To change mesh scale","To create a signal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ground input should use the horizontal projection of camera direction.","lessonId":"math-2"},{"id":"check-math-3","category":"3D Math for Action Games","type":"choice","prompt":"A dot product near -1 means the directions are:","options":["Nearly opposite","Nearly identical","Both zero","Different lengths only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Normalized directions pointing opposite produce -1.","lessonId":"math-3"},{"id":"check-math-4","category":"3D Math for Action Games","type":"choice","prompt":"Which function is designed for angles that wrap around?","options":["lerp_angle","print","emit_signal","queue_free"],"answer":0,"explanation":"lerp_angle handles angle wraparound correctly.","lessonId":"math-4"},{"id":"check-move-1","category":"Movement, Camera, and Lock-On","type":"choice","prompt":"Which node should own code-driven third-person collision movement?","options":["CharacterBody3D","Label","AudioStreamPlayer","WorldEnvironment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CharacterBody3D is designed for scripted character motion.","lessonId":"move-1"},{"id":"check-move-2","category":"Movement, Camera, and Lock-On","type":"choice","prompt":"Why separate acceleration and deceleration?","options":["Starting and stopping may need different feel","To create UVs","To enable signals","To change physics tick rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Separate rates let starts and stops have different responsiveness and weight.","lessonId":"move-2"},{"id":"check-move-3","category":"Movement, Camera, and Lock-On","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the job of a SpringArm3D in this setup?","options":["Help keep the camera out of geometry","Store attack damage","Retarget bones","Control enemy health"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A spring arm can shorten around obstructions.","lessonId":"move-3"},{"id":"check-move-4","category":"Movement, Camera, and Lock-On","type":"choice","prompt":"Why make break distance larger than acquisition distance?","options":["To create stable hysteresis","To increase attack damage","To stop all camera input","To reduce texture size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The gap prevents rapid lock/unlock near one threshold.","lessonId":"move-4"},{"id":"check-anim-1","category":"Animation Implementation","type":"choice","prompt":"What should control combat active timing?","options":["Authored combat data","The visual clip length alone","The monitor refresh rate","The mesh polygon count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gameplay timing must remain authoritative.","lessonId":"anim-1"},{"id":"check-anim-2","category":"Animation Implementation","type":"choice","prompt":"What input is most useful for a locked-on locomotion blend space?","options":["Character-local lateral and forward velocity","Raw screen pixels","Enemy health only","File paths"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Local velocity describes movement relative to the character’s facing.","lessonId":"anim-2"},{"id":"check-anim-3","category":"Animation Implementation","type":"choice","prompt":"What is wrong with waiting for an overly long landing animation to finish?","options":["It can block control long after the physical action is over","It improves responsiveness","It fixes collision automatically","It reduces memory use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Visuals should fit the gameplay action, not extend it arbitrarily.","lessonId":"anim-3"},{"id":"check-anim-4","category":"Animation Implementation","type":"choice","prompt":"When should a skeleton mismatch be discovered?","options":["As early as possible, before systems depend on it","After final combat polish","Only during export","It never matters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Early verification prevents expensive downstream rework.","lessonId":"anim-4"},{"id":"check-combat-1","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"choice","prompt":"Which phase makes a missed attack punishable?","options":["Recovery","Active only","Idle","Import"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recovery preserves commitment after a miss.","lessonId":"combat-1"},{"id":"check-combat-2","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"choice","prompt":"When should hit_targets be cleared?","options":["At the start of each new swing","Every frame","Only on death","Never"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each swing needs a fresh record while still preventing duplicate hits within that swing.","lessonId":"combat-2"},{"id":"check-combat-3","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"choice","prompt":"Why forbid most cancels during active hit frames?","options":["To preserve attack commitment and readable rules","To change texture filtering","To improve Wi-Fi","To create collision shapes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Active frames are the committed striking portion.","lessonId":"combat-3"},{"id":"check-combat-4","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"choice","prompt":"Without invulnerability frames, what makes a dodge successful?","options":["Actual displacement outside the attack","A hidden immunity flag","The dodge animation name","Enemy input reading"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The character must physically avoid the hit.","lessonId":"combat-4"},{"id":"check-ai-1","category":"Enemy AI","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the benefit of separating intention from execution?","options":["AI can use shared legal combat actions instead of cheating","It removes all states","It disables recovery","It makes attacks instantaneous"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Decision code chooses; shared combat code enforces rules.","lessonId":"ai-1"},{"id":"check-ai-2","category":"Enemy AI","type":"choice","prompt":"Which input is prohibited for fair AI?","options":["Player raw button input","Distance to player","Player visible attack state","Recent repeated action"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The AI should react to game state, not private raw input.","lessonId":"ai-2"},{"id":"check-ai-3","category":"Enemy AI","type":"choice","prompt":"What should happen before weighted selection?","options":["Remove actions that are not currently valid","Read future player inputs","Disable recovery","Set all weights equal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weighting should only choose among legal actions.","lessonId":"ai-3"},{"id":"check-ai-4","category":"Enemy AI","type":"choice","prompt":"Which debug information is most useful?","options":["State transition reason","Only frame rate","Texture file size","Monitor brightness"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The reason reveals why the AI chose or failed to choose an action.","lessonId":"ai-4"},{"id":"check-blend-1","category":"Blender Pipeline","type":"choice","prompt":"Which mode edits vertices and faces?","options":["Edit Mode","Object Mode only","Pose Mode only","Render Mode"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Edit Mode changes mesh geometry.","lessonId":"blend-1"},{"id":"check-blend-2","category":"Blender Pipeline","type":"choice","prompt":"Which property mainly controls how sharp or broad reflections appear?","options":["Roughness","Node name","Physics tick rate","Signal count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Roughness controls microsurface reflection spread.","lessonId":"blend-2"},{"id":"check-blend-3","category":"Blender Pipeline","type":"choice","prompt":"What does weight painting define?","options":["Bone influence on vertices","Camera collision range","Attack recovery","Controller deadzone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weights determine mesh deformation under bones.","lessonId":"blend-3"},{"id":"check-blend-4","category":"Blender Pipeline","type":"choice","prompt":"Which editor is best for adjusting animation curves and easing?","options":["Graph Editor","File browser","UV editor only","Godot Output panel"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Graph Editor exposes interpolation curves.","lessonId":"blend-4"},{"id":"check-debug-1","category":"Debugging and Production Discipline","type":"choice","prompt":"What should you usually fix first when many errors appear?","options":["The earliest error likely causing later failures","The last cosmetic warning","All files at once","The texture with the largest size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Later errors may be consequences of the first failure.","lessonId":"debug-1"},{"id":"check-debug-2","category":"Debugging and Production Discipline","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the goal of a minimal reproduction?","options":["Isolate the smallest setup that still fails","Add every production system","Improve art quality","Avoid all testing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Isolation reduces the number of possible causes.","lessonId":"debug-2"},{"id":"check-debug-3","category":"Debugging and Production Discipline","type":"choice","prompt":"Why inspect a diff before committing AI-generated changes?","options":["To understand exactly what changed and catch unrelated edits","To retarget animations","To increase frame rate","To create a mesh"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Diff review keeps generated changes controlled and understandable.","lessonId":"debug-3"},{"id":"check-debug-4","category":"Debugging and Production Discipline","type":"choice","prompt":"What makes an acceptance test useful?","options":["It describes observable action and expected result","It uses vague adjectives only","It avoids playtesting","It replaces version control"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Observable criteria make completion verifiable.","lessonId":"debug-4"},{"id":"p-code-01","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"predict","prompt":"What is printed?","code":"var speed: float = 6.0\nvar input_strength: float = 0.5\nprint(speed * input_strength)","answerText":"3.0","accepted":["3.0","3"],"explanation":"6.0 * 0.5 = 3.0. Godot 4 prints floats with the trailing decimal (3.0); Godot 3 printed 3."},{"id":"p-code-02","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"choice","prompt":"Find the safest correction for a possible null target.","code":"func face_target() -> void:\n    look_at(target.global_position)","options":["Add `if target == null: return` before look_at","Rename the function","Call queue_free first","Move it to Blender"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guard prevents invalid access when target is missing."},{"id":"p-code-03","category":"GDScript Foundations","type":"fill","prompt":"Fill the blank so movement is time-based.","code":"position += direction * speed * _____","accepted":["delta"],"answerText":"delta","explanation":"Multiplying by delta applies a per-second speed consistently."},{"id":"p-math-01","category":"3D Math for Action Games","type":"predict","prompt":"Player is at (2,0,3) and target is at (5,0,7). What is target - player?","answerText":"(3, 0, 4)","accepted":["3,0,4","(3,0,4)","(3, 0, 4)"],"explanation":"Subtract each component: 5-2, 0-0, 7-3."},{"id":"p-math-02","category":"3D Math for Action Games","type":"choice","prompt":"A normalized direction has length:","options":["1 unless it was the zero vector","0 always","Its original distance","60"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Normalization creates unit length while preserving direction."},{"id":"p-move-01","category":"Movement, Camera, and Lock-On","type":"choice","prompt":"Full-stick sprint fails only when the camera is pitched. What should you inspect first?","options":["Whether camera basis vectors are flattened and renormalized before combining input","Enemy health","Texture resolution","Animation bone count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pitch can reduce horizontal magnitude if vertical components are discarded without renormalization."},{"id":"p-anim-01","category":"Animation Implementation","type":"choice","prompt":"A jump lasts 0.75 s, but Jump_Start lasts 2.2 s and must finish in 0.25 s. What general solution fits the project rule?","options":["Scale animation playback speed to fit 0.25 s","Increase jump air time to 2.2 s automatically","Count animation frames in _process","Remove gravity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gameplay timing remains authoritative; retime the visual."},{"id":"p-combat-01","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"choice","prompt":"An attack uses wind-up 0.24, active 0.12, recovery 0.46. When does recovery begin?","options":["0.36 s","0.12 s","0.46 s","0.82 s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recovery starts after wind-up plus active: 0.24 + 0.12."},{"id":"p-combat-02","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"predict","prompt":"What is the total attack duration?","code":"windup = 0.24\nactive = 0.12\nrecovery = 0.46","answerText":"0.82 seconds","accepted":["0.82","0.82s","0.82 seconds"],"explanation":"Add all three phases."},{"id":"p-combat-03","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"choice","prompt":"The hitbox overlaps a target for six physics ticks and damage applies six times. What rule is missing?","options":["Per-swing hit tracking","A larger texture","A second camera","More gravity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Track targets already hit during the current swing."},{"id":"p-state-01","category":"Architecture and Communication","type":"choice","prompt":"The player is STAGGERED. Which request should normally fail?","options":["Start a new attack","Update the stagger timer","Play a stagger animation","Receive death if health reaches zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A staggered combatant should not begin a normal attack."},{"id":"p-ai-01","category":"Enemy AI","type":"choice","prompt":"Which AI design is fair?","options":["React when player attack state becomes visibly active","Read attack button before the attack begins","Ignore recovery after attacking","Teleport into range"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Visible combat state is legitimate information; private raw input is not."},{"id":"p-debug-01","category":"Debugging and Production Discipline","type":"choice","prompt":"A lock-on bug appears only in the full arena. What is the best next step?","options":["Build a minimal scene with player, one target, distances, and lock logic","Rewrite all camera and combat code","Add final art","Change engine versions immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Isolate the lock-on behavior before changing unrelated systems."},{"id":"p-order-01","category":"Debugging and Production Discipline","type":"order","prompt":"Put the debugging process in a useful order.","items":["Reproduce the issue","Collect evidence","Form one hypothesis","Run a focused test","Record the result"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"explanation":"Reproduction and evidence should precede a hypothesis and focused test."},{"id":"p-order-02","category":"Combat Engineering","type":"order","prompt":"Put a clean attack lifecycle in order.","items":["Clear per-swing hit list","Begin wind-up","Enable hitbox for active window","Disable hitbox","Complete recovery","Return to free state"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4,5],"explanation":"The hit list resets at attack start, then the timed phases progress."}],"missions":[{"id":"xogot-01","level":"Foundation","title":"First 3D Training Bay","duration":"20–35 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Create and run a clean 3D scene using only named nodes.","steps":["Create a Node3D root named TrainingBay.","Add a MeshInstance3D floor and a CollisionShape3D through a StaticBody3D.","Add a DirectionalLight3D and Camera3D.","Position the camera so the floor is visible.","Save as training_bay.tscn and run it."],"acceptance":["Scene launches without errors.","Floor is visible and lit.","Every node has a purpose-based name."],"hint":"Start with a large BoxMesh for the floor.","extension":"Add three primitive pillars as reusable scenes."},{"id":"xogot-02","level":"GDScript","title":"Controller-Driven Marker","duration":"30–45 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Move a simple Node3D using named input actions.","steps":["Create move_left, move_right, move_forward, and move_back actions.","Attach a script to a visible marker.","Read Input.get_vector.","Convert the Vector2 into a horizontal Vector3.","Move using speed * delta."],"acceptance":["Marker moves in four directions.","Diagonal movement is not faster than straight movement.","No physical controller button is read directly."],"starter":"func _process(delta: float) -> void:\n    var input := Input.get_vector(\"move_left\", \"move_right\", \"move_forward\", \"move_back\")\n    var direction := Vector3(input.x, 0.0, input.y)\n    position += direction * speed * delta","hint":"Input.get_vector handles a circular deadzone and diagonal limiting.","extension":"Expose speed with @export."},{"id":"xogot-03","level":"Movement","title":"Falling Capsule","duration":"35–55 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Build a minimal CharacterBody3D with gravity and floor collision.","steps":["Create a CharacterBody3D with capsule collision and visible mesh.","Create a static floor with collision.","Add gravity in _physics_process.","Call move_and_slide.","Print is_on_floor only when its value changes."],"acceptance":["Capsule falls and rests on the floor.","No continuous error spam.","Floor state becomes true when grounded."],"hint":"Use ProjectSettings.get_setting for default gravity or define an exported gravity value.","extension":"Add jump with a just-pressed action."},{"id":"xogot-04","level":"Architecture","title":"Reusable Health Component","duration":"35–50 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Create a health node that can be reused by multiple objects.","steps":["Create a Node script named HealthComponent.","Export max_health.","Initialize current_health.","Add take_damage and reset functions.","Emit damaged and died signals.","Attach it to two different target scenes."],"acceptance":["Both targets use the same component script.","Damage is clamped and death emits once.","Reset restores full health."],"starter":"signal damaged(amount: float)\nsignal died\n@export var max_health: float = 100.0\nvar current_health: float","hint":"Track whether death was already emitted.","extension":"Include final hit region in the died signal."},{"id":"xogot-05","level":"Signals","title":"Trigger, Door, Indicator","duration":"40–60 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Use signals to connect three separate objects without one giant script.","steps":["Create an Area3D trigger.","Create a Door scene.","Create an Indicator light or mesh.","Emit activated from the trigger.","Connect the signal to door and indicator behavior."],"acceptance":["Entering the trigger changes both objects.","Trigger script does not directly search for named children inside the door.","Behavior resets cleanly."],"hint":"Connect signals in the parent training scene for maximum visibility.","extension":"Add a reset button."},{"id":"xogot-06","level":"Data","title":"AttackData Resource","duration":"35–60 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Create a custom Resource for attack configuration.","steps":["Create attack_data.gd extending Resource.","Add class_name AttackData.","Export damage, windup, active, recovery, and animation_name.","Create light_attack.tres and heavy_attack.tres.","Print both resources from a test controller."],"acceptance":["Two attacks share one Resource class.","Values differ without duplicated controller code.","All timing is stored in seconds."],"starter":"class_name AttackData\nextends Resource\n\n@export var damage: float = 10.0\n@export var windup: float = 0.25","hint":"Resources appear as creatable types after the script parses successfully.","extension":"Add chain and cancel window fields."},{"id":"xogot-07","level":"Combat","title":"Visible Attack Timeline","duration":"45–70 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Run wind-up, active, and recovery using one timer.","steps":["Create an AttackData resource.","Start an attack on input.","Accumulate elapsed time in _physics_process.","Change a mesh color for each phase.","Return to idle after total duration."],"acceptance":["Phases happen in the correct order.","Changing the Resource changes timing.","Mashing does not restart the attack."],"hint":"Compare elapsed against cumulative boundaries: windup, windup+active, total.","extension":"Allow a queued second attack only inside a chain window."},{"id":"xogot-08","level":"Collision","title":"One Hit Per Swing","duration":"50–80 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Use Area3D contact without repeated damage.","steps":["Create a weapon Area3D and target hurtbox.","Configure layers and masks.","Enable monitoring only during active time.","Track already-hit areas.","Clear the list when a new attack starts."],"acceptance":["One swing damages once even across multiple physics ticks.","A second swing can damage again.","Weapon does not hit its owner."],"hint":"The body_entered/area_entered signal may fire once, but overlapping or multiple hurtboxes still require deliberate tracking.","extension":"Add head and torso hurtboxes with different multipliers."},{"id":"xogot-09","level":"State","title":"Action State Gate","duration":"40–65 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Prevent attack, dodge, and stagger from overlapping.","steps":["Create an ActionState enum.","Add can_start_action.","Enter ATTACKING on attack.","Reject dodge during active frames.","Enter STAGGERED from a forced test input.","Return to FREE after timers."],"acceptance":["Invalid actions are rejected.","Current state is visible on screen or Output.","Every temporary state has a defined exit."],"hint":"Centralize state changes in one function so transitions can be logged.","extension":"Create a transition table Resource or dictionary."},{"id":"xogot-10","level":"Camera","title":"Orbit Camera Rig","duration":"60–90 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Create yaw and pitch camera orbit with controller input.","steps":["Build yaw and pitch pivots.","Read look actions as analog values.","Apply yaw to the horizontal pivot.","Clamp pitch.","Use a SpringArm3D or ray-based collision solution."],"acceptance":["Full orbit works without rolling the camera.","Pitch stays within limits.","Camera does not pass through the test wall."],"hint":"Keep yaw and pitch rotations on separate nodes.","extension":"Add smoothing that still yields immediately to direct input."},{"id":"xogot-11","level":"Targeting","title":"Single-Target Lock-On","duration":"60–90 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Acquire one target and face it while preserving camera orbit.","steps":["Add a target group or direct target reference.","Validate acquisition range.","Toggle locked_target.","Rotate the character toward the target.","Break lock when the target is too far or dead."],"acceptance":["Lock toggles predictably.","Character faces target without snapping.","Right-stick orbit still works.","Break distance is larger than acquire distance."],"hint":"Flatten the direction before calculating horizontal facing.","extension":"Add soft camera framing bias."},{"id":"xogot-12","level":"AI","title":"Approach and Circle","duration":"60–90 min","device":"Xogot","objective":"Create enemy intention states without attacks.","steps":["Create INACTIVE, APPROACH, and CIRCLE states.","Activate inside a radius.","Approach when too far.","Circle at preferred range.","Display current state and distance."],"acceptance":["Enemy stays inactive before activation.","Enemy reaches preferred range.","State does not flicker rapidly at thresholds."],"hint":"Use separate enter and exit thresholds or a small cooldown.","extension":"Randomly reverse circle direction after a committed interval."},{"id":"design-01","level":"Design","title":"Design a Complete Light Attack","duration":"20–35 min","device":"Any phone","objective":"Create an implementation-ready attack specification without opening Godot.","steps":["Name the attack and its intended tactical role.","Set wind-up, active, and recovery durations.","Set damage, lunge, and movement influence.","Define chain and cancel windows.","Write five acceptance tests."],"acceptance":["Every number has a reason.","Windows do not contradict phase boundaries.","The attack can be represented by one Resource."],"hint":"Start from desired readability and commitment, not animation length.","extension":"Create a contrasting heavy attack."},{"id":"design-02","level":"Design","title":"Build a Combatant Node Tree","duration":"20–40 min","device":"Any phone","objective":"Design scene responsibilities before coding.","steps":["Choose a root node.","Add collision and visuals.","Add movement, attack, defense, health, and animation responsibilities.","Mark which pieces are reusable for enemy and player.","List public methods and signals."],"acceptance":["Player input is not inside shared health or attack data.","AI can request shared actions.","No component has unrelated responsibilities."],"hint":"Use the Node Tree Lab and export the result to notes.","extension":"Add equipment attachment nodes."},{"id":"design-03","level":"Debugging","title":"Write a Minimal Reproduction Plan","duration":"15–25 min","device":"Any phone","objective":"Prepare a disciplined test before returning to the laptop.","steps":["Write the exact observed behavior.","Write the expected behavior.","List the smallest required nodes.","List all systems to remove.","Define one measurement that distinguishes two hypotheses."],"acceptance":["The reproduction can be built in under 30 minutes.","The test can disprove at least one hypothesis.","No visual polish is included."],"hint":"A minimal reproduction is an experiment, not a miniature game.","extension":"Write a second test for the next most likely cause."}],"glossary":[{"term":"Node","definition":"One object in Godot with a specific role."},{"term":"Scene","definition":"A saved, reusable tree of nodes."},{"term":"Instance","definition":"A placed copy linked to a reusable scene."},{"term":"Inspector","definition":"Editor panel for node and Resource properties."},{"term":"Signal","definition":"An event emitted by one object and received by connected listeners."},{"term":"Resource","definition":"Serializable data object used for configuration and assets."},{"term":"CharacterBody3D","definition":"Code-driven character body with collision-aware movement."},{"term":"Area3D","definition":"Overlap-detection region commonly used for triggers, hitboxes, and hurtboxes."},{"term":"Collision layer","definition":"The category an object belongs to."},{"term":"Collision mask","definition":"The categories an object checks for."},{"term":"Transform","definition":"Position, rotation, and scale represented together."},{"term":"Basis","definition":"The oriented axes and rotation/scale portion of a Transform3D."},{"term":"Vector","definition":"A set of components representing position, direction, or velocity."},{"term":"Normalize","definition":"Change a vector to length 1 while preserving direction."},{"term":"Dot product","definition":"A scalar measurement of directional alignment."},{"term":"Delta","definition":"Elapsed time in seconds since the previous update."},{"term":"Physics tick","definition":"One fixed-rate physics update."},{"term":"State machine","definition":"A model of modes and allowed transitions."},{"term":"AnimationTree","definition":"Godot system for animation blending and state control."},{"term":"Blend space","definition":"A graph that blends animations based on one or more numeric values."},{"term":"Root motion","definition":"Movement stored in animation root tracks rather than entirely in code."},{"term":"Retargeting","definition":"Mapping animation from one skeleton to another."},{"term":"Wind-up","definition":"Telegraphing phase before an attack can hit."},{"term":"Active window","definition":"Time during which an attack hitbox can produce a hit."},{"term":"Recovery","definition":"Committed time after an attack before ordinary actions resume."},{"term":"Cancel window","definition":"Explicit time when an action may transition into another action."},{"term":"Hitbox","definition":"Collision volume that represents an attack."},{"term":"Hurtbox","definition":"Collision volume that can receive a hit."},{"term":"Hysteresis","definition":"Different enter and exit thresholds used to prevent rapid boundary switching."},{"term":"Minimal reproduction","definition":"The smallest setup that still demonstrates a bug."},{"term":"Acceptance test","definition":"Observable action and expected result used to verify a feature."},{"term":"PBR","definition":"Physically based rendering material workflow."},{"term":"UV map","definition":"2D coordinates mapping a texture onto a 3D mesh."},{"term":"Armature","definition":"Bone hierarchy used to pose and animate a mesh."},{"term":"Weight painting","definition":"Assignment of bone influence across mesh vertices."}],"labs":[{"id":"attack","title":"Attack Timeline Lab","summary":"Design wind-up, active, recovery, chain, and cancel windows and export a Resource-ready specification."},{"id":"defense","title":"Defense Reflex Lab","summary":"Practice recognizing perfect, standard, and failed defense timing."},{"id":"vector","title":"Vector & Facing Lab","summary":"Move player and target points to inspect direction, distance, normalized vectors, and dot product."},{"id":"collision","title":"Collision Matrix Lab","summary":"Configure which combat layers detect one another and test for common mistakes."},{"id":"ai","title":"AI Decision Lab","summary":"Adjust distance, health, and action weights to see which intention wins."},{"id":"tree","title":"Node Tree Architect","summary":"Construct a scene hierarchy and export it into your workshop notes."},{"id":"code","title":"GDScript Scratchpad","summary":"Write, save, and copy phone-sized code drafts and pseudocode for later use in Xogot or Godot."}]};
